The solder points on the back of the board are actually solderable jumpers. It is not intended that wiring be soldered here, but purely for selecting the voltage.

By default, the module is set to provide a variable voltage via the potentiometer and can be adjusted as desired. With a magnifying glass, you can see that the last jumper (ADJ) is soldered. If you want to use one of the fixed voltages (1.8V, 2.5V, 3.3V, 5V, 9V or 12V), the connection at ADJ must be broken. This can be cut with a small sharp knife. Then solder a connection at the desired voltage.

